# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there. MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST carry --swa-full —
# without it every Claude Code turn re-processes the whole prompt.
MODEL_TITLE=Coding agent · 68.35 GiB · predecessor, kept as the way back
MODEL_SWA=yes
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched, so a sharded quant comes down whole and an
# mmproj rides along. Read by setup/get-model.sh, which is this stack's
# equivalent of `ollama pull` — except that the list it offers is MEASURED on
# this hardware (bash setup/lib/models.sh table) rather than a generic catalog.
# Verified 26.08. by matching the exact filename in the repo, not by guessing.
MODEL_SOURCE=unsloth/Laguna-S-2.1-GGUF UD-Q4_K_XL
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — measured. Read by setup/lib/budget.py.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=96.0
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=23.08.2026, the note that set -c 131072 for both models: Gemma 4 needs 1.3 GiB of KV at 131k and Laguna 12 GiB. 12 GiB over 131,072 cells is 96.0 KiB per token.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Coding agent · 68.35 GiB of weights, 3 parts · start on part 1, it finds the rest
# CORRECTED: this said -00001-of-00002, there really are 3 parts
# Speculative decoding disabled: laguna-s-2.1-DFlash-BF16.gguf does not exist in
#   the repo, and eagle3 makes output measurably 2.2-2.7x slower (15 % acceptance).
#
# -np 2  : Claude Code sends TWO prompt types per session — the main request
#          (19.4k tokens) and a title generation.
#          -np also decides how many PROJECTS stay warm at the same time:
#          every working directory is its own prefix (it appears in two places
#          in the system prompt). Measured with --swa-full:
#              -np 2   2 projects warm   65,536 tokens per project
#              -np 4   4 projects warm   32,768 tokens per project
#          Both carry at 100 % (8 of 8 queries). Memory does NOT change, because
#          --no-kv-unified only slices the same -c pot differently.
#          Anyone going to -np 4 has to move CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS in
#          ~/.claude/profiles/local.json to 32768 as well. At 32,768 only ~13k
#          are left for the work after the 19.4k system prompt.
#          The statement noted here earlier, "with more than two the cache falls
#          apart", only held without --swa-full.
# --no-kv-unified : splits -c evenly across the slots, i.e. 65536 per slot.
#          That matches CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=65536 in the profile.
#
# --swa-full : DO NOT LEAVE OUT. Laguna has sliding window attention
#          (laguna.attention.sliding_window = 512). Without this switch
#          llama.cpp can only roll the KV state back within 512 tokens.
#          Claude Code appends a 1,624-token block behind the user question
#          ("Available agent types for the Agent tool: ..."), so every changed
#          question lies far outside the window. The server then discards the
#          entire prefix:
#              forcing full prompt re-processing due to lack of cache data
#          Measured on the real Claude Code body, changed question:
#              without --swa-full   new=19,371  cache=0        100.2 s
#              with    --swa-full   new= 1,637  cache=17,734    10.4 s
#          Memory: 82.8 of 96 GiB GTT (73.2 before). Fits.
#
# NOTE:    The statement noted here earlier, that without cc-cachefix.py every
#          request costs ~140 s, was right — the reasoning was not. It was not
#          the system message as such but the fact that it sits behind the user
#          question and pushes the point of divergence out of the SWA window.
#          With --swa-full the proxy is no longer needed for the cache; it stays
#          useful only for removing thinking/context_management/output_config
#          (otherwise 400).
# -cram WAS 32768, and it did not fit. Found 27.08. by setup/lib/budget.py the
# first time anything computed a whole profile instead of its weights:
#
#     weights                              68.4 GiB
#     KV at -c 131072, 96.0 KiB/token      12.0
#     compute buffers and loader (10 %)     8.0   -> 88.4 pinned in GTT
#     RAM prompt cache, worst case         32.0
#     host share                           12.0
#                                         ------
#                                          132.4 of 124.9
#
# Both numbers in that sum are MEASURED, so this is not an estimate refusing a
# configuration it does not understand: the profile promised 7.5 GiB more than
# the machine has. It survived only because the cache never filled — the same
# sentence the -cram audit wrote about Flash-Next on 27.08., about a different
# profile, one day earlier. The audit fixed the profile it was looking at.
#
# 16384 leaves 116.4 of 124.9. What the cache loses is a re-prefill on an
# evicted prefix; what the old number risked is a machine that stops
# responding, because GTT is pinned and does not page.
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias laguna -m @MODELS@/Laguna-S-2.1-UD-Q4_K_XL-00001-of-00003.gguf \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -ub 512 -b 2048 -c 131072 -np 2 -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0 \
  --no-kv-unified -cram 16384 --swa-full \
  --slot-save-path @HOME@/.cache/llama-slots \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080

# Backend for this profile. Measured 24.08. (docs/MODELS.md): the choice
# is MODEL-dependent — Vulkan wins for models without speculation, ROCm wins
# 2x once MTP/ngram speculation is on. Inert until llama-user@.service is
# switched to: ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'exec "$HOME/$LLAMA_BIN" $LLAMA_ARGS' (one sudo install).
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-vulkan/bin/llama-server
